{"question": "Pregabalin có nên được sử dụng thường quy làm thuốc giảm đau chính sau phẫu thuật nhổ răng khôn hay không?", "claim": "Pregabalin có lợi ích giảm đau rõ rệt sau phẫu thuật răng khôn và cân bằng lợi ích - nguy cơ hoàn toàn ủng hộ việc sử dụng thường quy loại thuốc này.", "internal_evidence": [{"evidence_id": "I1", "content": "The effect of pregabalin for pain after third molar surgery: a systematic review and meta-analysis\nBody > Discussion\nFrom a clinical perspective, the modest analgesic benefit should be weighed against the increased risk of adverse events. Although pregabalin reduced postoperative pain by approximately 1 point on the VAS scale, this effect was near the lower boundary of the MCID and became statistically non-significant in sensitivity analyses after exclusion of Diniz et al. (2024). In contrast, the increased risk of adverse events was more consistent, with a pooled OR of 4.36 and preservation of statistical significance across leave-one-out analyses. Therefore, the benefit–harm balance appears uncertain and may not favor routine use of pregabalin as a primary analgesic after third molar surgery, particularly when effective and well-established alternatives such as NSAIDs and corticosteroids are available."}], "conversation": {"history": []}, "public_quote_options": [{"quote_id": "P1.Q1", "evidence_id": "P1", "text": "because active comparators, such as ibuprofen or corticosteroid-containing regimens, may reduce the apparent incremental benefit of pregabalin. However, the persistence of high heterogeneity within the placebo plus dexamethasone subgroup indicates that comparator type alone does not fully explain the inconsistency, and other factors, including timing of pain assessment, surgical variability, analgesic rescue protocols, and repeated postoperative measurements, may also have contributed."}, {"quote_id": "P2.Q2", "evidence_id": "P2", "text": "From a clinical perspective, the modest analgesic benefit should be weighed against the increased risk of adverse events. Although pregabalin reduced postoperative pain by approximately 1 point on the VAS scale, this effect was near the lower boundary of the MCID and became statistically non-significant in sensitivity analyses after exclusion of Diniz et al. (2024). In contrast, the increased risk of adverse events was more consistent, with a pooled OR of 4.36 and preservation of statistical significance across leave-one-out analyses. Therefore, the benefit–harm balance appears uncertain and may not favor routine use of pregabalin as a primary analgesic after third molar surgery, particularly when effective and well-established alternatives such as NSAIDs and corticosteroids are available."}], "public_sources": [{"evidence_id": "P1", "title": "The effect of pregabalin for pain after third molar surgery: a systematic review and meta-analysis", "heading_path": ["Body", "Discussion"]}, {"evidence_id": "P2", "title": "The effect of pregabalin for pain after third molar surgery: a systematic review and meta-analysis", "heading_path": ["Body", "Discussion"]}]}
