{"question": "Liệu pháp quang sinh học (photobiomodulation) làm giảm nguy cơ tương đối mắc viêm niêm mạc miệng nặng theo thang đo WHO là bao nhiêu phần trăm?", "claim": "Các nghiên cứu sử dụng thang đo WHO ghi nhận liệu pháp quang sinh học giúp giảm nguy cơ tương đối mắc viêm niêm mạc miệng với giá trị RR là 0,24.", "internal_evidence": [{"evidence_id": "I1", "content": "Tài liệu nội bộ ghi nhận kết quả phân tích phân nhóm theo thang đo WHO cho thấy RR là 0,24 (KST 95%: 0,05 đến 1,07)."}], "conversation": {"history": []}, "public_quote_options": [{"quote_id": "P1.Q1", "evidence_id": "P1", "text": "Twelve effects across 11 studies were included in the model for oral mucositis. A total of 274 cases of severe mucositis were reported among 707 patients [2, 4, 18, 26–29, 37, 43, 49–51, 55, 59], corresponding to a cumulative incidence of 38.8%. Severe mucositis was assessed using different grading systems (WHO, RTOG, and NCI), which are broadly comparable in identifying clinically significant mucositis but differ in thresholds and clinical interpretation. Subgroup analysis by mucositis grade assessment indicated statistically significant differences (χ2 = 7.8, P = 0.020). Studies using the WHO scale (RR 0.24, 95% CI: 0.05 to 1.07, P = 0.206) and RTOG scale (RR 0.34, 95% CI: 0.22 to 0.53, P = 0.020) displayed greater reductions in mucositis relative risk compared with those using the NCI staging (RR 0.65, 95% CI: 0.32 to 1.29, P = 0.150)."}, {"quote_id": "P2.Q2", "evidence_id": "P2", "text": "Thirty-three articles describing 30 RCTs (n = 1748) were included. Photobiomodulation significantly reduced the risk of severe oral mucositis (RR 0.46, 95% CI: 0.29–0.71, P < 0.001) and severe oral pain (RR 0.35, 95% CI: 0.23–0.53, P < 0.001) compared with controls. Significant improvements were also observed for salivary flow rate (SMD 0.75, 95% CI: 0.03–1.46, P = 0.044). No significant effects were observed on xerostomia (SMD −0.07, 95% CI: −0.47–0.33, P = 0.646) and quality of life (SMD 1.06, 95% CI: −0.03–2.14, P = 0.060). The benefits were consistent across different photobiomodulation protocols for oral mucositis and quality of life."}], "public_sources": [{"evidence_id": "P1", "title": "Effects of photobiomodulation on oral mucositis, oral pain, xerostomia, salivary flow rate, and quality of life in patients with head and neck cancer: a systematic review and meta-analysis", "heading_path": ["Body", "Results", "Oral mucositis"]}, {"evidence_id": "P2", "title": "Effects of photobiomodulation on oral mucositis, oral pain, xerostomia, salivary flow rate, and quality of life in patients with head and neck cancer: a systematic review and meta-analysis", "heading_path": ["Abstract", "Results"]}]}
