{"question": "Mối liên hệ giữa triệu chứng đầy tai và rối loạn khớp thái dương hàm (TMD) như thế nào sau khi điều trị TMD?", "claim": "Có tới 90,2% bệnh nhân báo cáo khỏi hoàn toàn hoặc cải thiện đáng kể triệu chứng đầy tai sau khi được điều trị rối loạn khớp thái dương hàm (TMD).", "internal_evidence": [{"evidence_id": "I1", "content": "Tài liệu nội bộ ghi nhận nhiều biểu hiện của hệ thống nhai có mối liên hệ mật thiết với các rối loạn ở cơ hàm và khớp thái dương hàm."}], "conversation": {"history": []}, "public_quote_options": [{"quote_id": "P1.Q1", "evidence_id": "P1", "text": "In a 2017 prospective cohort study, 112 patients with a presumed non-otolaryngological origin of aural fullness were evaluated. These patients exhibited signs and symptoms of TMD and were treated according to disease categories and therapeutic responsiveness. Among the study participants, 90.2% reported either complete resolution or significant improvement of aural fullness following TMD treatment. TMD therapies, especially physical therapy, proved highly effective in patients with muscle-related TMD (Table 2). However, treatments were often ineffective in those with structural temporomandibular joint changes. This study established a correlation between aural fullness and TMD in the patient cohort [14]."}, {"quote_id": "P2.Q2", "evidence_id": "P2", "text": "Most studies utilized established standardized frameworks, either the RDC/TMD or the DC/TMD. A total of 57 RCTs used DC/TMD, while 72 used older RDC/TMD. The adoption of DC/TMD was associated with more precise subtype stratification, especially in distinguishing myofascial pain from arthralgia. Studies using multi-axial frameworks were more likely to incorporate Axis II psychosocial assessments and reported better alignment between diagnosis and targeted intervention outcomes. See Supplementary Table 2 Data Extraction."}], "public_sources": [{"evidence_id": "P1", "title": "Otologic Manifestations of Temporomandibular Disorders", "heading_path": ["Body", "4. Discussion", "4.1. Aural Fullness", "4.1.1. Overview and Etiology"]}, {"evidence_id": "P2", "title": "Diagnosis-based pathways for conservative and minimally invasive management of temporomandibular disorders: a scoping review", "heading_path": ["Body", "3. Result", "3.2 Characteristics of included studies"]}]}
